# Print specs

## Purpose

Fix the physical geometry of the page before any page is drawn: where the
paper is cut, how far art must run past the cut, where text is safe, and how
many pixels that takes.

## Scope

Applies only when `comic_format` in `production/project.yaml` is `paged`.
Governs the `page` block of `project.yaml` (`aspect_ratio`, `width_px`,
`height_px`, `trim_mm`, `bleed_mm`, `safe_margin_mm`), the placement of
lettering and important art on every page, and the preparation of a `print`
deliverable. Sits on top of `rules/common/` and the domain layers without
contradicting them.

## Core Principles

- Page geometry is set once, at project init, from the intended printed
  size. Every page is planned and generated to it.
- Three boundaries matter: the trim, where the paper is cut; the bleed,
  beyond the trim; and the safe area, inside it.
- Cutting is imprecise. Anything near the trim may be lost, and anything
  that stops exactly at the trim may leave a white sliver.
- Print-ready files are prepress work. The scaffold supplies correct pages
  and exact numbers, and a prepress tool makes the press file.

## Trim, bleed, and safe margin

- `page.trim_mm` is the size of the finished page. A common comic-book trim
  is 168 × 260 mm. Use the printer's number when a printer is chosen.
- `page.bleed_mm` is how far art extends beyond the trim on every outer
  edge. Default: 3 mm. Some printers ask for 5.
- `page.safe_margin_mm` is the distance inside the trim within which
  nothing important is placed. Default: 6 mm.
- A panel that is meant to reach the edge of the paper (a bleed panel)
  extends through the full bleed. A panel that is not meant to reach the
  edge stops at the safe line or inside it. No panel border sits between the
  safe line and the trim.
- A project that sets none of these fields is a screen-only project. Add
  them before a `print` deliverable is declared.

## Resolution and pixel size

- Target 300 pixels per inch at the printed size for tonal and color art.
  Pure black-and-white line art benefits from 600 and is commonly scaled up
  by the prepress tool.
- `page.width_px` and `page.height_px` follow from the trim: 168 × 260 mm at
  300 ppi is 1984 × 3071 px, and with 3 mm of bleed on each edge, 2055 ×
  3142 px.
- `page.aspect_ratio` SHOULD match the trim proportions as closely as the
  provider's supported ratios allow. `2:3` is the nearest common ratio to
  most comic trims. When ratio and trim differ, the prepress step states
  how the difference is resolved (which edge is cropped, or where paper is
  left blank), and important art keeps clear of the affected edge.
- When a provider cannot return the target size, generate at the correct
  ratio and upscale in prepress. Record the real pixel size of each render,
  and do not report a page as 300 ppi when it is not.
- Convert millimetres to pixels with the project's own numbers: at 300 ppi,
  3 mm is 35 px and 6 mm is 71 px.

## Lettering inside the safe area

- Every balloon, caption, and sound effect that has to be read sits wholly
  inside the safe area: at least `safe_margin_mm` from the trim on all
  sides.
- The same holds for faces, hands, and held props the story tracks. Art
  that can lose a few millimetres (sky, floor, background) fills the space
  between the safe line and the bleed edge.
- In `in_image` mode the prompt states the margin, and the review checks
  the pixels. In `overlay` mode the `box` of every element in
  `lettering.yaml` is placed inside the safe area, measured in pixels on
  the overlay's `canvas`.
- Sound effects drawn as part of a bleed panel may run off the page when
  losing their outer letters does no harm to the reading.

## Spine and gutter loss

- The inner edge of a bound page curves into the spine. A perfect-bound
  book hides roughly 3 to 6 mm of each page there, and more as the book
  gets thicker. A saddle-stitched booklet loses less.
- Default inner margin: keep lettering, faces, and hands at least 10 mm
  from the inner edge of every page in a perfect-bound book, in addition to
  the safe margin on the other three sides.
- On a double-page spread, art crosses the fold and detail does not. No
  lettering, face, or hand lies within 10 mm of the fold on either page.
- The two halves of a spread can shift against each other by a millimetre
  or two in binding. Avoid thin diagonal lines, small objects, and
  horizon-level detail that would show the step.
- Which edge is the inner edge follows the page's `side` and the project's
  `reading_direction` (see `page-turn-grammar.md`).

## Color and ink

- `color_mode` and `spot_colors` in `project.yaml` describe the artwork.
  Converting it for press (CMYK separation, ink limits, a true black for
  line art, spot plates) is done in prepress to the printer's profile.
- A `monochrome` project keeps its blacks solid and its whites clean. Grey
  texture that the style bible does not define is a style defect and may
  print as noise.
- Review printed proofs when the project can afford them. A screen does not
  show how heavy blacks fill in on paper.

## What the scaffold does not do

- It does not add crop marks, bleed boxes, or color profiles, and it does
  not impose pages into printer's spreads.
- The PDF from `scripts/build-edition.js` is a reading copy
  (`publishing/export-formats.md`).
- The prepress-specialist prepares the press file in an external tool from
  the renders of one named edition, and records the tool, the settings, and
  the printer's specification in the edition's `notes`.

## Validation

- `schemas/project.schema.json` requires `page.aspect_ratio`, `width_px`,
  and `height_px`, and checks the types of `trim_mm`, `bleed_mm`, and
  `safe_margin_mm` when present.
- Agreement between pixel size, trim, bleed, and the 300 ppi target is
  arithmetic on `project.yaml`. The prepress-specialist does it at project
  init and again before a `print` deliverable, and no script does it today.
- In `overlay` mode the letterer compares each `box` in `lettering.yaml`
  with the safe margin in pixels. In `in_image` mode only the picture
  shows where the text landed, so margin compliance is part of the
  `lettering` check of the visual review.
- Faces and hands near the trim or the fold are judged on the rendered page
  under `drawing_clarity`, and the meeting edge of a spread by comparing
  both halves side by side.
- The press file itself is checked by the prepress-specialist against the
  printer's specification, and by a proof when one is ordered.
